    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. just to be absolutely sure on the documentation side, could you expand a bit on why that specific two-stage cooling window is so critical for preventing spoilage? i need to explain the rationale to my kitchen staff so they take the monitoring seriously.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The two-stage cooling window is critical because it effectively manages the \"Danger Zone,\" which is where bacteria multiply fastest. If you cool too slowly, the poultry spends too much time in that temperature range, allowing the *Clostridium perfringens* spores to germinate and multiply to dangerous levels. The initial rapid drop to 41°F (5°C) is the most aggressive phase, stopping the most vigorous growth immediately. This rapid reduction minimizes the time the food is susceptible to harmful bacterial proliferation.",
      "source": "target",
